BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:transmediale-pro-helvetia-digital-art-residency-2027@artificialnouveau.
 github.io
DTSTAMP:20260810T194932Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260828
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260829
SUMMARY:Deadline: transmediale x Pro Helvetia Digital Art Residency 2027
DESCRIPTION:The fifth iteration of transmediale's Digital Art Residency\, r
 un with the Swiss Arts Council Pro Helvetia\, awarded to one individual or
  collective working in digital art practices at the intersection of media 
 arts\, performance\, sound and moving image. THEME: the 2027 programme sit
 s under transmediale's 40th-anniversary framework RFC 40\, named after the
  early internet Request for Comments series\, and treats the festival as a
  counter-infrastructure: the call asks how a network stays legible to itse
 lf while surrounding structures break down\, and how technical paradigms c
 an be reconfigured into new cultural protocols outside extractive logics\,
  moving from critique to construction. Proposals should offer alternatives
  to how we understand\, respond to and shape the technological conditions 
 we inhabit. WHAT IS OFFERED: an artist fee of CHF 2\,000 per month for two
  months\; a CHF 15\,000 production grant supporting a new work or the cont
 inuation of an existing project\, open to all formats and building on the 
 research begun during the residency (the project must be completed by Dece
 mber 2027 at the latest)\; travel and accommodation for the festival and t
 he residency\; a shared modular working space at transmediale studio\; con
 nections to Berlin institutions and stakeholders\; a public presentation i
 n transmediale's year-round studio programme\; and the option of an online
  publication in transmediale's journal. STRUCTURE: kick-off with a present
 ation of the residency projects during transmediale 2027 (26 to 31 January
  2027\, attendance optional but strongly recommended)\; onboarding and the
  first hybrid stretch in May 2027\; the mandatory on-site month in Berlin 
 in June 2027\; and a second hybrid stretch including reporting in July 202
 7. Availability for the festival week and for the whole of June in Berlin 
 are treated as key prerequisites. TIMELINE: open call 16 July to 28 August
  2026 (23:59 CEST)\; selected residents announced October 2026. Applicatio
 ns are only accepted via transmediale's submission forms. Contact: info[at
 ]transmediale.de.\n\nOrganisation: transmediale (in cooperation with the S
 wiss Arts Council Pro Helvetia)\nLocation: Hybrid: remote plus on-site at 
 transmediale studio\, silent green Kulturquartier\, Berlin-Wedding\, Germa
 ny. RESTRICTED: applicants must hold Swiss nationality or permanent reside
 nce in Switzerland and be firmly established in the Swiss cultural scene (
 Pro Helvetia's general requirements apply).\nAward: Artist fee of CHF 2\,0
 00 per month across the two-month residency (CHF 4\,000)\, plus a producti
 on grant of CHF 15\,000 for the selected project\, plus travel and accommo
 dation for both the festival and the residency month. Duos and collectives
  may apply but the fee and the production grant stay the same and must be 
 shared. No application fee.\nDuration: Two-month hybrid residency between 
 mid-May and mid-July 2027\, with the whole of June 2027 on-site in Berlin 
 (mandatory)\n\nMore: https://transmediale.de/en/news/digital-art-residency
 -2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://transmediale.de/en/news/digital-art-residency-2027?utm_source=a
 rtificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Hybrid: remote plus on-site at transmediale studio\, silent green 
 Kulturquartier\, Berlin-Wedding\, Germany. RESTRICTED: applicants must hol
 d Swiss nationality or permanent residence in Switzerland and be firmly es
 tablished in the Swiss cultural scene (Pro Helvetia's general requirements
  apply).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:transmediale-vilem-flusser-residency-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260810T194932Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260828
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260829
SUMMARY:Deadline: Vilem Flusser Residency for Artistic Research 2027
DESCRIPTION:14th iteration of the residency collaboration between transmedi
 ale and the Vilem Flusser Archive at the Universitat der Kunste Berlin (Ud
 K)\, awarded to one Berlin-based artist-researcher working in digital art 
 and moving image practices\, design\, sound and media theory. Open to arti
 sts\, researchers\, practitioners\, designers\, filmmakers and sound artis
 ts across all formats. The two-month residency comes with an artist fee of
  EUR 1\,500 per residency month\; note there are NO funds for travel or ho
 using. Duos and collectives are welcome but share the same fee. Part of tr
 ansmediale's 2027 programme (40th anniversary\, themed 'RFC 40')\, seeking
  proposals that offer alternatives to how we understand\, respond to and s
 hape our technological conditions\, moving from critique to construction. 
 Deadline: 28 August 2026\, 23:59 CEST.\n\nOrganisation: transmediale and t
 he Vilem Flusser Archive\, Universitat der Kunste Berlin (UdK)\nLocation: 
 Berlin\, Germany (open to Berlin-based artist-researchers)\nAward: Two-mon
 th residency with an artist fee of EUR 1\,500 per residency month. NO fund
 s are available for travel or housing. For duos or collectives the fee rem
 ains the same and is shared. No application fee.\nDuration: Two-month resi
 dency\n\nMore: https://transmediale.de/en/news/vilem-flusser-residency-for
 -artistic-research-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=r
 eferral
URL:https://transmediale.de/en/news/vilem-flusser-residency-for-artistic-re
 search-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Berlin\, Germany (open to Berlin-based artist-researchers)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:movida-labs-dance-technology-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260810T194932Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260803
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260804
SUMMARY:Deadline: MOVIDA: Labs on Dance and Technology 2026/27
DESCRIPTION:MOVIDA is a Creative Europe co-funded consortium pairing academ
 ic institutions with dance centres across four European cities. Sixteen du
 os\, each made up of one dancer and one creative technologist\, are select
 ed across four themed labs: AI and accessibility in Bucharest\, AI and AR/
 VR in Dusseldorf\, AI and robotics in Lisbon\, and AI and mental health in
  Tallinn. Applicants are assessed on demonstrated capability in integratin
 g technology with live performance\, computational creativity\, and audien
 ce interaction. Participants selected for the labs receive fast-track cons
 ideration for the MOVIDA residencies running in 2027 and 2028.\n\nOrganisa
 tion: MOVIDA (Movement and Virtual Intelligence for the Development and Ac
 cessibility of Artists and Audiences)\nLocation: Four labs across Europe: 
 Bucharest (Romania)\, Dusseldorf (Germany)\, Lisbon and Montemor-o-Novo (P
 ortugal)\, and Tallinn (Estonia). Applications are accepted only from duos
  of one dancer and one creative technologist\; you cannot apply solo. Both
  members must reside in an EU or Creative Europe eligible country\, so Net
 herlands-based applicants qualify.\nAward: EUR 1\,200 gross per artist (EU
 R 2\,400 per duo)\, plus travel and accommodation covered\, plus a product
 ion budget of up to EUR 500 per duo. Accessibility and personal-assistance
  costs are covered by separate agreement. No application fee.\nDuration: N
 ine days per lab\, scheduled between 3 December 2026 and 11 February 2027\
 n\nMore: https://movida.dance/news/call-labs/?utm_source=artificialnouveau
 grantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://movida.dance/news/call-labs/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantd
 esk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Four labs across Europe: Bucharest (Romania)\, Dusseldorf (Germany
 )\, Lisbon and Montemor-o-Novo (Portugal)\, and Tallinn (Estonia). Applica
 tions are accepted only from duos of one dancer and one creative technolog
 ist\; you cannot apply solo. Both members must reside in an EU or Creative
  Europe eligible country\, so Netherlands-based applicants qualify.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:francoise-cite-internationale-arts-clinique-2027@artificialnouveau.gith
 ub.io
DTSTAMP:20260810T194932Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260816
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260817
SUMMARY:Deadline: Association Francoise pour l'oeuvre contemporaine x Cite 
 internationale des arts: Research and Creation Residency 2027
DESCRIPTION:Launched in 2025\, this research and creation residency explore
 s the links between contemporary art\, psychiatry and the human sciences. 
 It is expressly not a therapeutic programme. It seeks artists whose practi
 ce resonates with the notion of the clinical\, meaning a situated approach
  that works closely with lived narratives and specific contexts\, and that
  engages with questions of care. The selected artist receives tailored sup
 port from Albertine de Galbert\, a clinical psychologist and exhibition cu
 rator\, including four to six working sessions\, alongside networking and 
 meeting time with programme partners. Outputs include an ongoing online jo
 urnal\, a mid-residency open studio\, and a closing presentation (exhibiti
 on\, public talk or publication) defined with the artist. Applications go 
 by email as a single PDF of no more than 15 MB to residence@francoiseartme
 mo.fr\, and require a two-page CV\, a ten-page portfolio\, a two-page stat
 ement of intent\, and a letter of recommendation from an institution or a 
 curator. A jury of four to six art and psychiatry specialists decides\, wi
 th results announced in September 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Association Franc
 oise pour l'oeuvre contemporaine / Cite internationale des arts\nLocation:
  Paris\, France (Marais site of the Cite internationale des arts). Open to
  artist-authors of any nationality and any discipline\, mid-career or esta
 blished\, with no age limit. Applicants must be comfortable working in Fre
 nch or English. Note: the studio-apartments do not meet current accessibil
 ity standards for people with disabilities.\nAward: Grant of EUR 1\,000 ne
 t of tax per month\, plus a furnished studio-apartment (20 to 35 sq m\, co
 mbining living and working space)\, international return travel to Paris a
 irports or stations\, travel insurance\, and capped research support again
 st receipts. Additional fees apply for accompanying persons. No applicatio
 n fee.\nDuration: Approximately 12 weeks\, 5 January to 26 March 2027\n\nM
 ore: https://www.citeinternationaledesarts.fr/appels-a-candidature/associa
 tion-francoise-pour-loeuvre-contemporaine-x-cite-internationale-des-arts/?
 utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.citeinternationaledesarts.fr/appels-a-candidature/associati
 on-francoise-pour-loeuvre-contemporaine-x-cite-internationale-des-arts/?ut
 m_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Paris\, France (Marais site of the Cite internationale des arts). 
 Open to artist-authors of any nationality and any discipline\, mid-career 
 or established\, with no age limit. Applicants must be comfortable working
  in French or English. Note: the studio-apartments do not meet current acc
 essibility standards for people with disabilities.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:waag-trust-me-im-an-airtist-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260810T194932Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: Trust me\, I'm an AI/rtist: Open Call for Creative Interv
 entions
DESCRIPTION:Waag Futurelab invites artists\, theatre makers\, choreographer
 s\, dancers\, game designers and other creative practitioners to engage di
 rectly with seven experimental generative AI Enablers being co-created wit
 h technologists and dance\, theatre and gaming professionals. The call is 
 an invitation into the development process: selected artists get access to
  the technologies\, datasets\, interfaces and assumptions that shape these
  systems while they are still being built. Rather than testing whether the
  systems work\, artists are asked to interrogate why they work the way the
 y do\, who they are designed for\, what forms of creativity they enable or
  constrain\, and what assumptions about culture\, intelligence\, authorshi
 p\, labour\, bodies\, audiences and society are embedded in them. Waag is 
 looking for individuals and collectives willing to move beyond adoption in
 to investigation\, and welcomes proposals that are speculative\, critical\
 , poetic\, provocative\, humorous\, performative\, constructive or disrupt
 ive\, including work that challenges\, misuses\, reimagines\, hacks\, subv
 erts or plays with the tools\, exposes hidden biases\, questions environme
 ntal costs\, or imagines alternative technological futures. THE SEVEN AI E
 NABLERS\, each requiring an on-site visit to its technology partner: the G
 eneric Procedural Content Generation Enabler (text-to-3D assets and avatar
 s\, CERTH\, Greece)\; the Data-Driven Cultural Content Adaptation Enabler 
 (audience-aware creative content ideation with ranked candidate ideas\, CE
 RTH\, Greece)\; the Cross-Domain Audience Engagement Analytics Enabler (tu
 rning heterogeneous cultural participation data into interpretable engagem
 ent signals\, The Audience Agency\, Warrington\, UK)\; the Dance Style Ana
 lysis and Generation Enabler (dance video into virtual 3D choreography\, d
 issected into body\, effort\, shape and space components\, Centre of Excel
 lence for Research & Innovation\, Cyprus)\; the Immersive Design Integrati
 on Enabler for Dance & Theatre (NLP-driven XR environments with speech-to-
 3D scene editing\, Cyprus)\; the Music-to-Movement Translation Enabler (au
 dio into a time-aligned feature set spanning genre classification\, emotio
 nal valence-arousal analysis\, rhythm extraction and latent-space projecti
 on\, feeding motion generation\, spatial audio and visual choreography\, A
 ristotle University of Thessaloniki\, Greece)\; and the Adaptive Narrative
  Enabler for Games & Theatre (an evolutionary AI trained on creator-writte
 n story elements that generates infinite story variations\, explicitly rej
 ecting LLMs because of their dependence on copyrighted or public-domain co
 rpora that encode 19th and early 20th century sexism\, racism and imperial
 ism\, CAUSA Creation\, Austria). ELIGIBILITY: artists\, designers\, creati
 ve technologists\, theatre makers\, dancers\, choreographers and game desi
 gners\, as individuals or collectives\; applicants must hold a European VA
 T number. An info session with live demonstrations of the AI Enablers runs
  31 July 2026\, 10:00 to 11:30 CEST (meeting ID 995 7016 2341). Applicatio
 ns go through Homerun using the official Application Template\, uploaded a
 s a PDF\; read the Guide for Applicants\, which contains the full Enabler 
 descriptions\, before applying. Questions: zoenie at waag dot org. The AI 
 Enablers and this call are part of the Horizon Europe project HAMLET\, whi
 ch aims to make generative AI accessible to cultural organisations of all 
 sizes. Closes 30 September 2026\, 23:59 CEST. Free to apply.\n\nOrganisati
 on: Waag Futurelab (Horizon Europe project HAMLET)\nLocation: Europe. Enga
 ging with a specific AI Enabler includes an on-site visit to its technolog
 y partner: CERTH (Greece)\, The Audience Agency (Warrington\, UK)\, the Ce
 ntre of Excellence for Research & Innovation (Cyprus)\, Aristotle Universi
 ty of Thessaloniki (Greece)\, or CAUSA Creation (Austria). Final presentat
 ions September 2027.\nAward: EUR 20\,000 commission fee excluding VAT\, in
 clusive of artist fee\, travel\, accommodation and production costs. Appli
 cants must have a VAT number in Europe. No application fee.\nDuration: Jan
 uary to September 2027\, with final presentations in September 2027\n\nMor
 e: https://waag.org/en/article/open-call-creative-interventions-trust-me-i
 m-airtist/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://waag.org/en/article/open-call-creative-interventions-trust-me-i
 m-airtist/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Europe. Engaging with a specific AI Enabler includes an on-site vi
 sit to its technology partner: CERTH (Greece)\, The Audience Agency (Warri
 ngton\, UK)\, the Centre of Excellence for Research & Innovation (Cyprus)\
 , Aristotle University of Thessaloniki (Greece)\, or CAUSA Creation (Austr
 ia). Final presentations September 2027.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:martin-roth-initiative-relocation-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260810T194932Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260911
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260912
SUMMARY:Deadline: Martin Roth-Initiative: Temporary Relocation for Artists 
 and Cultural Actors at Risk (Programme Line 1\, 2026 call)
DESCRIPTION:The Martin Roth-Initiative is the German temporary-relocation p
 rogramme for artists and cultural actors at risk\, run jointly by the Goet
 he-Institut and ifa with Federal Foreign Office funding. Programme Line 1 
 relocates an artist to Germany for up to 15 months on a scholarship\, with
  insurance\, language courses\, psychosocial support\, counselling and vis
 a assistance\, plus personnel funding for the hosting organisation. ELIGIB
 ILITY: artists and cultural practitioners anywhere in the world who are de
 monstrably committed through their work to an open society\, freedom\, pea
 ce and democracy\; who face threats\, persecution or unsafe living conditi
 ons\; who have no access to a safe place to continue working\; and who spe
 ak English or German. Applicants must still be active in their home countr
 y or have left it after 20 February 2026. Citizens of EU and EEA countries
 \, Australia\, Canada\, Israel\, New Zealand\, Switzerland\, the UK and th
 e USA are excluded\, as are residents of Gaza and previous MRI recipients.
  HOST REQUIREMENT: applications are submitted jointly with a non-profit ho
 st organisation in Germany (museum\, theatre\, festival\, cultural collect
 ive\, NGO) that is a legal entity able to handle the administrative and di
 gital-security requirements. Note that the scholarship visa does not itsel
 f permit employment or self-employment in Germany. TIMELINE: applications 
 6 July to 11 September 2026\; decisions end of November 2026\; funding sta
 rts no earlier than 15 January 2027. Apply to apply@mri-application.de (en
 crypted email recommended)\; phone +49 30 284491-77.\n\nOrganisation: Mart
 in Roth-Initiative (Goethe-Institut and ifa\, funded by the German Federal
  Foreign Office)\nLocation: Germany (relocation to a German host organisat
 ion). Open to artists and cultural actors worldwide\, with exclusions.\nAw
 ard: Monthly scholarship for the relocated artist\, plus insurance\, langu
 age courses\, psychosocial support\, counselling and training\, visa suppo
 rt\, and personnel funding for the host organisation. No application fee.\
 nDuration: Up to 15 months\, starting no earlier than 15 January 2027\n\nM
 ore: https://www.martin-roth-initiative.de/en/application-programme-line-1
 ?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.martin-roth-initiative.de/en/application-programme-line-1?u
 tm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Germany (relocation to a German host organisation). Open to artist
 s and cultural actors worldwide\, with exclusions.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:julies-bicycle-iter-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260810T194932Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: ITER 2026: International Touring and Environmental Respon
 sibility Programme
DESCRIPTION:ITER is now in its fifth year and supports people in the perfor
 ming arts to build environmentally responsible\, inclusive and equitable w
 ays of touring and collaborating internationally. It is initiated and fina
 nced by Arts Council England\, Arts Council Norway\, Creative Scotland and
  the Danish Arts Foundation\, and designed and delivered by Julie's Bicycl
 e and in futurum. Creative Scotland joined as a partner for 2026. TWO TRAC
 KS\, up to 60 places in total: PATHFINDERS\, ten sessions introducing the 
 principles of sustainable touring for people new to the subject\; and CHAN
 GEMAKERS\, nine sessions for those further along\, on leadership and syste
 mic change. Seminars run weekly from 1 October to 10 December 2026\, Pathf
 inders on Tuesdays and Changemakers on Thursdays\, 10:00 to 12:00 UK time\
 , alongside networking and a new alumni network. THE MONEY COMES IN PHASE 
 TWO: after the seminars\, groups of three or more participants can apply f
 or up to GBP 30\,000 to implement collaborative projects that cut environm
 ental impacts and advance climate justice. Funding applications open 6 Nov
 ember 2026 and close 11 January 2027\; awarded projects begin in spring 20
 27. ELIGIBILITY: based in England\, Scotland\, Norway or Denmark\, working
  in dance\, music or theatre. That covers music and theatre venues receivi
 ng touring work\, festivals\, orchestras\, theatre companies\, solo artist
 s\, performers and musicians\, performing arts producers\, and multidiscip
 linary arts organisations. Applications are made through the relevant nati
 onal funder (Arts Council England open funds\, Creative Scotland targeted 
 funding\, Arts Council Norway\, Danish Arts Foundation). Questions go to e
 nvironmentaltouring@artscouncil.org.uk.\n\nOrganisation: Julie's Bicycle a
 nd in futurum\, funded by Arts Council England\, Arts Council Norway\, Cre
 ative Scotland and the Danish Arts Foundation\nLocation: England\, Scotlan
 d\, Norway and Denmark. Seminars run online.\nAward: The seminar programme
  is free to join. Groups of three or more participants can then apply for 
 up to GBP 30\,000 for collaborative projects\, with funding applications o
 pen 6 November 2026 to 11 January 2027 and projects starting spring 2027. 
 No application fee.\nDuration: Seminars run weekly from 1 October to 10 De
 cember 2026\, Tuesdays for Pathfinders and Thursdays for Changemakers\, 10
 :00 to 12:00 UK time. Deadline to join 1 September 2026.\n\nMore: https://
 juliesbicycle.org/news/iter-2026/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&ut
 m_medium=referral
URL:https://juliesbicycle.org/news/iter-2026/?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:England\, Scotland\, Norway and Denmark. Seminars run online.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:spazioserra-open-call-2026-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260810T194932Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260906
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260907
SUMMARY:Deadline: Open Call 2026/2027 'Where is the tomorrow we saw yesterd
 ay?' (spazioSERRA)
DESCRIPTION:spazioSERRA turns ten in 2027\, and this open call is framed as
  a deliberate break from how independent art usually asks artists to arriv
 e: with deadlines met\, ideas already defined\, and a project ready to be 
 exhibited. The call asks 'where is the tomorrow we saw yesterday?' and sta
 tes plainly that it is not looking for completed projects or stabilised vi
 sions\, but for practices\, processes\, obsessions and still-open question
 s. Site-specific installations\, performances\, editorial productions\, vi
 deos\, urban interventions\, hybrid collaborations and forms that are stil
 l undefined are all welcome\; the point is not the format but the urgency 
 running through it\, and the willingness to enter a shared research proces
 s that can slow down\, change direction and stay incomplete long enough to
  transform. WHAT IT LEADS TO: the call identifies artists\, collectives an
 d research practices with whom spazioSERRA may develop collaborations duri
 ng the 2027 exhibition and project season\, which may take the form of sol
 o or group exhibitions\, site-specific installations\, performances\, edit
 orial productions\, video projects\, collaborations with institutions or e
 xternal organisations\, and further projects developed together. If select
 ed\, the artist or collective begins a curatorial dialogue with spazioSERR
 A aimed at developing an exhibition and/or additional projects\, jointly d
 efined during the collaboration. FUNDING: each selected project receives a
  financial contribution of EUR 500 to EUR 2\,000\, determined by the proje
 ct's characteristics and available resources\, covering production costs o
 f the work (which remains the property of the artist or collective)\, trav
 el\, food and accommodation\, and communication and promotion materials. T
 he contribution is paid at the end of the collaboration unless otherwise a
 greed\, and spazioSERRA reimburses only documented expenses paid by tracea
 ble methods with the relevant tax documentation. It is explicitly a contri
 bution towards the realisation of the project and/or the practice\, not ne
 cessarily full coverage of production costs\; if the project is developed 
 with external partners bringing additional resources\, the budget may be s
 upplemented by mutual agreement. Use of the exhibition space and the work 
 of the spazioSERRA team cost the artist nothing. SELECTION: no exhibition 
 project or defined proposal is required\; the jury assesses the artistic r
 esearch as a whole\, including the applicant's path\, the languages used\,
  the questions explored\, the potential for development and compatibility 
 with spazioSERRA's curatorial vision. The selecting curatorial collective 
 is Pietro Agostini\, Lidia Barca\, Tracy Bassil\, Virginia Dal Magro\, Mas
 similiano Fanto\, Camilla Gurgone\, Giulia Maffioli Brigatti\, Cristiano R
 izzo\, Victoria Tincati and Valentina Toccaceli. First selection in Novemb
 er 2026\, interviews November to December 2026\, final notification by Dec
 ember 2026. APPLICATION: online form open 15 July to 6 September 2026\, wi
 th a single PDF containing statement\, artistic CV and a selection of work
 s\, shared via a stable online link viewable without download\, authorisat
 ion or password\; collectives list all members. FEE: EUR 12 membership fee
  per person\, multiplied by the number of members for a collective. Contac
 t: info.spazioserra@gmail.com.\n\nOrganisation: spazioSERRA\nLocation: Mil
 an\, Italy. spazioSERRA operates inside the Milano Lancetti passante railw
 ay station\; registered office Via Maloia 1\, 20158 Milan. Open to artists
  and collectives of any nationality\, aged 18 or over.\nAward: EUR 500 to 
 EUR 2\,000 financial contribution per selected project\, set according to 
 the project's characteristics and available resources\, paid at the end of
  the collaboration against documented expenses with traceable payment meth
 ods. No cost to the artist for use of the exhibition space or for the work
  of the spazioSERRA team. Application fee: EUR 12 membership fee per perso
 n (multiplied by the number of members for collectives).\nDuration: Collab
 orations developed across the 2027 exhibition and project season\n\nMore: 
 https://www.spazioserra.org/en/exhibitions/open-call-2026-2027?utm_source=
 artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.spazioserra.org/en/exhibitions/open-call-2026-2027?utm_sour
 ce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Milan\, Italy. spazioSERRA operates inside the Milano Lancetti pas
 sante railway station\; registered office Via Maloia 1\, 20158 Milan. Open
  to artists and collectives of any nationality\, aged 18 or over.
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
